29cbb82e [skid@archlinux] can i set a recent date to now - 120 days and use that as optional cutoff when refining
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 6.070KB 5.69s
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #Yes. Since every line's leading hex mark is just a count of 62.5 ms intervals from `epoch`, you can
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #convert a "now - N days" cutoff into the same units and compare before emitting the line. Make it
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #optional by only applying it when a positive day count is supplied (e.g. via `Deno.args`).
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #Sketch of the changes:
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #const cutoffDays=Deno.args.length>0?parseInt(Deno.args[0]):0;
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #const cutoffSixteenths=cutoffDays>0
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #	?Math.floor((Date.now()-cutoffDays*86400000-epoch)/62.5)
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #	:0;
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #Then inside `reduce`, after you compute `mark`:
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #const mark=parseInt(trim2.substring(0,s1),16);
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #if(cutoffSixteenths>0&&mark<cutoffSixteenths){
29cbb89f [deepseek-flash] #	count++;
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #	continue;
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #}
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #const date=slopDate(mark);
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #Notes:
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #- Place the check before `slopDate(mark)` so skipped lines don't influence `minDate`/`maxDate`.
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #- `count++` keeps the existing "skipped" accounting consistent; drop it if you'd rather those lines
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #be invisible in the summary.
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #- If you also want the header/vendor/model statistics to reflect only the retained window, move the
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #cutoff check above the `parseHeader` / tag handling block instead.
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #- Invoke with `deno run -A refine.ts 120` for a 120-day window, or with no arg for full history.
29cbb8a0 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbc41 [skid@archlinux] what is 8640000?????
29cbbca5 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 7.524KB 5.82s
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #The number I used is 86,400,000 (`86400000`), not `8640000`.
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #- `86,400,000` = milliseconds in one day
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #  `24  60  60 * 1000 = 86,400,000`
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #- `8,640,000` = `86,400,000 / 10`, which is 2.4 hours
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #So `cutoffDays * 8640000` would be 10× too small. For `cutoffDays = 120` you’d get 12 days instead
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #of 120.
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #To avoid counting zeros, define it as a named constant:
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #const MS_PER_DAY = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #const cutoffSixteenths = cutoffDays > 0
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #	? Math.floor((Date.now() - cutoffDays * MS_PER_DAY - epoch) / 62.5)
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #	: 0;
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #Or with numeric separators, which TypeScript/Deno accept:
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #const MS_PER_DAY = 86_400_000;
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #That makes it clear it’s 86 million, not 8.6 million.
29cbbca6 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 14.13KB 8.30s
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #The cutoff works, but it sits a bit late in the loop. Two things to be aware of:
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #• 1. `slopSeconds` updates `minDate`/`maxDate` before the cutoff check*
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #const secs=slopSeconds(mark);   // <- mutates min/max
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #if(secs<recent){
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #	continue;                    // too late, range already widened
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #}
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #const date=slopDate(mark);
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #So the final `"usage from X to Y"` line reports the range of the raw input, not of what
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #actually landed in `history/`. If you want the report to describe the refined window, compute the
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #seconds without touching min/max until after the check. That also removes the need for two
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #near-identical functions:
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #function slopTime(sixteenths:number):Date{
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #	return new Date(epoch+sixteenths*62.5);
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #}
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc546 [deepseek-flash] #// in the loop
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #const mark=parseInt(trim2.substring(0,s1),16);
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #const date=slopTime(mark);
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #if(date.getTime()<recent){
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #	continue;
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #}
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #minDate=Math.min(minDate,mark);
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #maxDate=Math.max(maxDate,mark);
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #result.push(format(date,"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")+trim2.substring(s1));
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #• 2. Stats are also outside the window*
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #`sessionCount`, `failCount`, `vendors` and `models` are all populated before the cutoff check, so
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #the histograms at the end cover the full raw history rather than the last 120 days. If you want the
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #report to match the file, the cutoff has to be tested before the `[roha] [FORGE]` header parsing
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #and tag handling. That requires reading the mark first — the mark is at the start of every line, so
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #you can parse it up front and `continue` early. The header detection is positional though (it flips
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #`inHeader` based on seeing `[roha] [FORGE]`), so skipping a header line would also change how the
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #following lines are classified. Worth deciding whether you care about that coupling before
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #moving the check.
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #• Minor*
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #- `bigCount` and the local `m` in `parseHeader` are unused.
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #- `recent` reads like a date but is a millisecond timestamp; `recentMillis` or `cutoff` is clearer.
29cbc547 [deepseek-flash] #- If you still want it optional, gate it on `Deno.args` as you asked before:
