29ceee4b [deepseek-flash] #Yes. In nano:
29ceee4b [deepseek-flash] #
29ceee4b [deepseek-flash] #• Ctrl + \\* (that’s `Ctrl` + backslash) opens “Search (to replace)”.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #1. Type the text to find, press Enter.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #2. Type the replacement, press Enter.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #3. Nano asks `Replace this instance?` — press A to replace All.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #   - `Y` replaces just this one.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #   - `N` skips to the next.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #   - `Ctrl + C` cancels.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #Notes:
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #- It’s literal text matching by default, not regex. No `\n` or `.*`.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #- It searches from the cursor position forward, then wraps around, so it covers the whole file
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #either way.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #- If `Ctrl + \` doesn’t reach nano (some terminals grab it), use Alt + R as the alternative
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #binding, or `Esc` then `R`.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #- Undo is Alt + U (or `Esc` then `U`) if you get it wrong. Redo is Alt + E.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #- Check nano’s bottom bar — it lists `^R Replace` when a replace prompt is active, and the version
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #number tells you if you have an old build. `nano --version`.
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #
29ceee4c [deepseek-flash] #If your replacement needs patterns (regex, column reordering, only lines starting with X), nano
