2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #Add it as a compile definition on the libjasper target. Two options depending on how the target is
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #defined.
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #• If it's a normal target:*
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #target_compile_definitions(jasper PRIVATE JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1)
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #• If you don't know the exact target name*, or want it to apply regardless:
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] ## Global fallback - affects every target in the project
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #add_compile_definitions(JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1)
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a378 [deepseek-flash] #Or via the old flag-style, which also works:
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS "${CMAKE_C_FLAGS} -DJAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1")
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #• What `PRIVATE` means here*
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #- `PRIVATE` — only libjasper's own sources see the define. This is what you want; it's an internal
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #config macro.
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #- `PUBLIC` — also propagates to anything linking libjasper. Unnecessary here.
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #- `INTERFACE` — only consumers, not libjasper itself. Not what you want.
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #• Watch out for a generated `config.h`*
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #Libjasper upstream is autotools-based and normally writes `JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H` into a generated
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #`jas_config.h` / `config.h`. If that header already exists and has:
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #/* #undef JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H */
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #then your `-D` on the command line and the `#undef` in the header will fight, and the header
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #usually wins if it's included after the definition. In that case, either:
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #1. Edit the generated header directly, or
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #2. Check whether `jas_stream.c` guards the include with `#ifdef JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H` and whether that
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #header is actually included before it — if not, the define is moot and you still need the raw
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #`#include <unistd.h>` patch I mentioned earlier.
2a18a379 [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #To confirm it took effect:
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #cmake --build . --verbose 2>&1 | grep jas_stream
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #You should see `-DJAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H=1` on the compile line for `jas_stream.c`.
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #Also note: `JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H` only helps if the source actually tests for it. Many libjasper
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #versions just do an unconditional `#include <unistd.h>` guarded by nothing, in which case the
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #define changes nothing and the real fix is the source edit. Worth grepping:
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #grep -rn "JAS_HAVE_UNISTD_H" /home/skid/nitrologic/pipeline/libjasper/
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #If that returns nothing, the define is pointless — patch the include instead.
2a18a37a [deepseek-flash] #
