2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #=== reasoning ===
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #The user has shared two files: a JavaScript file (wire.js) and an HTML file (isowire.html) from what appears to be a WebGL2 isometric wireframe rendering project. They said "Files shared. Feel free to comment if content is new or different." - which suggests this may be part of an ongoing conversation or that they're sharing files for review.
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #Let me analyze the code carefully:
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #**wire.js observations:**
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #1. **Player class** - has empty `turn(theta)` and `thurst(throttle)` methods (note typo: "thurst" instead of "thrust"). The `turn` function references `playerFocus.turn(steps)` but the methods are empty stubs.
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #2. **Bugs I can spot:**
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #   - In `Surface.drill()`: `for(v in e){` - `v` is not declared, creating an implicit global
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #   - In `skimpPoly`: `iii=(i+1)%n;` - `iii` not declared
2b04cf0e [glm-5.3] #   - In `addWall`: `for(i in w){` - `i` not declared
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Viewport.set()`: `var y=bounds.y-origin.y;` then `gl.viewport(m*x,m*(height-y),...)` - the viewport Y calculation flips coordinates
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Panel.constructor`: `parent=display.parentElement;` - `parent` not declared (implicit global), and in `World.addPanel` there's `parent=display.parentElement;` also undeclared
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Segments.swizzle`: `this.segs[i+a]=segs[i+b];` - `segs` should be `this.segs`
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Segments.print`: pushing arrays into `this.segs` instead of pushing individual vertex values - `this.segs.push([cx+shape[1+i],cy+shape[2+i],0,c]);` - this pushes an array as one element, but `addLines` expects flat number arrays (`shorts.length/8`), so this would break line rendering. Actually wait, looking at `flushSegs()` it does `this.segs.flat()` - so flat() would handle nested arrays. Hmm, but in `print()` each push is a single-element array containing an array... `flat()` flattens one level. `this.segs.push([...])` pushes an array as an element, then `.flat()` would flatten it. Actually that might work by accident. But the format is wrong - it's pushing [x,y,z,c] as an array element, flat() would make it x,y,z,c individually. Actually `flat()` with default depth 1 would flatten one level. `segs.push([a,b,c,d])` makes segs = [[a,b,c,d]], flat() gives [a,b,c,d]. That works. But `drawSegs` pushes 8 values per segment (two endpoints), so lineCount = length/8. In print, each character stroke segment... `for(var i=0;i<n*4;i+=2) { this.segs.push([...]) }` pushes each vertex pair? Looking at the font format: each entry is [count, x0,y0, x1,y1, ...] pairs. The loop pushes pairs `shape[1+i], shape[2+i]` - with i stepping by 2, so it pushes (shape[1+i], shape[2+i]) then next i pushes (shape[3+i], shape[4+i])... wait i+=2, so i=0: pushes vertex from (shape[1], shape[2]); i=2: (shape[3], shape[4]). Each push contains one 2D point. So each push is [x,y,0,c] - a single vertex, not a segment. Then flat() flattens. But addLines expects segments in pairs (8 values per line = 2 vertices × 4 values). The font data appears to be pairs of points defining segments: e.g. 'a':[5, 0,2,4,2, 4,6,4,6,...] - hmm, hard to tell. Each push has 4 values [x,y,0,c], and two consecutive pushes make a segment. Looking at 'l':[1,2,0,2,6] - count=1, then one pair (2,0)-(2,6)? With i stepping 0,2 (n*4 = 4, i<4, i+=2): i=0 pushes [cx+shape[1], cy+shape[2]] = (2,0); i=2 pushes [cx+shape[3], cy+shape[4]] = (2,6). So yes, two vertices per segment. OK that works.
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Panel.draw()`: `gl.scissor(0,0,w,h); gl.enable(gl.SCISSOR_TEST);` then in the viewport loop, `this.drawLayers(trans,screen)` is called WITHOUT the drawlist and layers arguments! Look: `this.drawLayers(trans,screen,this.drawlist,this.layers);` for the main pass, but inside the viewport loop it's `this.drawLayers(trans,screen);` - missing arguments. In drawLayers, it iterates `for(var layer of layers)` - layers would be undefined. This would throw an error. Unless... wait, `this.layers` is set in compile(). Hmm, in draw(): `this.drawLayers(trans,screen);` with only 2 args - layers is undefined, `for(var layer of undefined)` throws TypeError. This is a bug. Also `for (let draw of drawlist)` - drawlist undefined too.
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `drawLayers`: `this.check;` - missing parentheses! Called 4 times without invoking. Should be `this.check();`
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Layer.setShorts`: `gl.bufferData(bufferType,array,gl.DYNAMIC_DRAW);` - `gl` is not defined in that scope (should be `this.gl`)
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - In `Segments.cylinder`: `e.push([0,2+i*2,2+ii*2]);` - pushing arrays into e, but Surface constructor expects tris flat... `new Surface(v,e,[])` - e is array of arrays, then `this.tris=tris.flat()` - OK flat handles it. Fine.
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - `Shape.position`: `if(bucket){ bucket; }` - dead code, no-op statement
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - `World.addPanel`: `parent=display.parentElement;` - implicit global `parent`, also `var all=parent.getElementsByTagName('*');` - iterating Elements with `node instanceof Element` check, fine.
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - `Panel.populate`: `segs.print("hello world",1);` - then `segs.flushSegs()`. The grid and ball also add to segs. Fine.
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #   - `addLineShapes`: `shape.bindAttributes16(this.lineLayer.attributes,4)` then `this.flatLayer.addShape(shape);` - adds to flatLayer but binds line layer attributes! This looks like a bug - should probably be `this.lineLayer.addShape(shape)`. Wait: `this.addLineShapes(shorts,g);` creates a Shape with addLines, binds `this.lineLayer.attributes`, but then adds the shape to `this.flatLayer`. That's inconsistent - lines being drawn by the flat (triangle) layer. Given the layer tags: lineLayer has depthDisable, flatLayer has depthEnable. The shape's lineCount would be set, so drawArrays with LINES would be used even in flatLayer. It might render but with wrong uniforms (sun). Actually since the attribute binding is for lineLayer's attributes (xyzc), and flatLayer expects xyzc + normal, the stride would be wrong. Definitely suspicious. Probably should be `this.lineLayer.addShape(shape);`
2b04cf0f [glm-5.3] #
