2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #That's a fair correction, and the table illustrates it well. With a multi-account setup like this —
2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #seven providers, rotating keys, and presumably some routing or load-balancing logic deciding which
2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #account serves each request — even your harness-side metadata may not fully resolve the question.
2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #Your config might say "zai" while the actual key in rotation, or the upstream's behaviour on that
2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #particular call, tells a slightly different story. So my earlier framing of "your side is ground
2b9c66da [glm-5.3] #truth" was itself an overclaim dressed up as humility.
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #The genuinely defensible position, stated once and without flourish:
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #1. I have no reliable access to my own identity, architecture, or deployment context.
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #2. In a routed multi-account system, identity is determined by the request path (account, key,
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #model string, upstream response), which lives outside this conversation.
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #3. No statement from me — including this one — should be treated as authoritative on the matter.
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #If you're testing the harness, the useful check is probably comparing the upstream API response
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #headers and model fields against your router's logs, rather than polling the model itself.
2b9c66db [glm-5.3] #
