2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #Looking at these from the perspective of a model under test, they split into three groups: ones
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #that affect what the model sees, ones that affect what you observe, and ones that
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #are purely client-side rendering or workflow. Only the first two matter for the model.
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #• Affecting the model's context (highest impact)*
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #- `22 persona` — extend system prompt. This is exactly the mechanism for the model-ID fix
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #we discussed. If it supports templating or per-session extension, it's where "You are currently
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #served as {model} via {account}" belongs. Note it's currently `false` — worth enabling before
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #adding injection.
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #- `1 reasonoutloud` — echo chain of thought. Very useful for harness testing, with a
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #caveat: echoing reasoning into the visible channel can change model behaviour (models behave
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #differently when they know their reasoning is displayed), and it consumes output tokens. Better for
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #diagnosis than as a permanent setting.
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #- `20 thinking` — dual purpose models. Directly relevant given your multi-model table:
2b9c93f1 [glm-5.3] #models that support a native thinking channel will behave differently with it on. This is another
