2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #Thanks. This is a compact custom-label Unicode table rather than a verbatim official-name listing.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #The main concern is `26Fx`:
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- `⛲`, `⛱`, and `⛽` each appear twice in the same JSON object. Many parsers silently keep only the
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #last value, discarding the earlier labels.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- `♨`, `⚓`, and `⚔` are outside U+26F0–U+26FF. The latter two also duplicate entries in `269x`,
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #which matters if the rows are flattened into one lookup.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- Six code points from the block are missing: U+26F4 ferry, U+26F7 skier, U+26F8 ice skate, U+26F9
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #person with ball, U+26FE cup on black square, and U+26FF white flag with horizontal middle black
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #stripe.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #- If official Unicode names are intended, `⛶` is “square four corners,” `⛻` is “Japanese bank
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #symbol,” and `⛼` is “headstone graveyard.” Also, `⛣` is not the Japanese bank symbol.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #The “variant” entries currently use identical character keys, not distinct variation-selector
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #sequences, so they cannot reliably coexist as written. I’d correct `26Fx` before using this as a
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #lookup table.
2bac0674 [kimi-k3.1-pro] #
